# The card's radios come up BLOCKED on every boot — asserted here, not
# inherited. This is a separate rules file from 99-dashberry.rules on
# purpose: it is the one rule firstinstall removes, on the single build
# that is meant to boot live (a debug card given a --wifi profile).
#
# Why a udev rule and not install-time state: before this rule, the boot
# state of a JOIN WIFI card (RF_JOIN=1 — the one production build that
# keeps its regulatory domain, so nothing hard-blocks wlan) rested
# entirely on NetworkManager re-applying the WirelessEnabled=false that
# firstinstall persisted. That is a single link, and a late one — the
# kernel registers the wlan rfkill switch with soft=0 and NM does not
# start for seconds afterwards. Worse, it is INHERITED state: the panel's
# `rf-ctl down` only ever runs when a user asks for it, so a card whose
# power was cut mid-JOIN-WIFI had nothing re-establish the block, and came
# back up RF-ENABLED. Setting the switch as it appears depends on no saved
# state, no daemon and no clean shutdown, so a crash cannot skip it.
#
# ACTION=="add" is load-bearing: an rfkill state change also reaches udev,
# and an unqualified rule would re-block the radios underneath a live
# JOIN WIFI session the instant rf-ctl unblocked them. Only the switch
# APPEARING (boot, or a driver rebind) may force it down; rf-ctl's
# explicit `rfkill unblock wifi` during a session is untouched.
#
# wlan only. The Bluetooth switch is a separate rfkill device and is not
# in rev 8's scope — the panel's glyph reads type wlan and nothing else.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="rfkill", ATTR{type}=="wlan", ATTR{soft}="1"
